t-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#334155\" class=\"st\">Start</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"172\" width=\"180\" height=\"38\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#e5e7eb\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"197\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#1f2937\" class=\"sb\">The dog ran.</text><text x=\"88\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#1e40af\" class=\"st\">Where?</text><g class=\"ln1\"><rect x=\"100\" y=\"216\" width=\"360\" height=\"38\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#dbeafe\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"241\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#1e40af\" class=\"sb\">The dog ran across the park.</text></g><text x=\"88\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#9a3412\" class=\"st\">When?</text><g class=\"ln2\"><rect x=\"100\" y=\"260\" width=\"470\" height=\"38\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#ffedd5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#9a3412\" class=\"sb\">After lunch, the dog ran across the park.</text></g><text x=\"88\" y=\"324\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#166534\" class=\"st\">What kind?</text><g class=\"ln3\"><rect x=\"100\" y=\"304\" width=\"560\" height=\"38\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#dcfce7\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"329\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#166534\" class=\"sb\">After lunch, the brown dog ran across the park.</text></g></g></svg>",
        "alt": "Animated cartoon: a dog runs across a park with stepping legs and a wagging tail while the sentence grows one line at a time and labels ask where, when and what kind.",
        "caption": "Watch the dog run while the sentence grows, one detail at a time."
      },
      "example": "Start: The dog ran.\nWhere? The dog ran across the park.\nWhen? After lunch, the dog ran across the park.\nWhat kind? After lunch, the brown dog ran across the park.",
      "notice": "Common mistake: adding so many words that the main idea is buried. Add detail one piece at a time and read the sentence again after each addition.",
      "check": {
        "question": "Which sentence uses detail most usefully?",
        "options": [
          "The dog ran.",
          "The brown dog ran across the park after lunch.",
          "The dog ran ran ran."
        ],
        "correct_index": 1,
        "explanation": "It tells what kind of dog, where it ran and when, and it still says one clear idea."
      }
    },
    {
      "icon": "👂",
      "title": "Check it and improve it",
      "explain": "Good writers do not finish a sentence once and move on. They check it. Read each sentence aloud, slowly, because your ear often hears a problem that your eye misses. Then run three checks. First, does it start with a capital letter and end with an end mark? Second, can you find who or what and what they did? Third, does it make complete sense on its own?\n\nIf a check fails, fix that one thing and read the sentence again. Work on one problem at a time, because changing many things at once makes it hard to tell what helped. Draft after school we played becomes After school, we played cricket in the yard once the capital letter, comma and missing detail are added.\n\nChecking and improving is part of writing, not a sign that you made a mistake. Every writer drafts and then fixes.",
